    questionText: "What are the crucial functions of empirical political theory in Comparative Politics?",
    modelAnswer:
      "Empirical political theory studies political phenomena as they actually ARE -- through systematic observation, data collection and causal analysis -- rather than prescribing what political arrangements ought to be, and it performs several crucial functions specifically within Comparative Politics.\n\nKey functions: (i) Description -- it systematically documents political institutions, processes and behaviour across countries (electoral systems, party structures, executive-legislative relations), providing the factual basis comparative analysis requires. (ii) Classification -- it enables typologies (e.g., parliamentary vs presidential systems, federal vs unitary states, party-system classifications) that organise diverse political realities into comparable categories. (iii) Explanation -- through hypothesis-testing and causal-inference methods, it identifies WHY political outcomes (democratic stability, regime transitions, policy variation) differ across countries, moving beyond mere description to causal understanding. (iv) Prediction -- empirically grounded models allow forecasting of likely political outcomes under specified conditions (e.g., predicting democratic breakdown risk from structural indicators). (v) Theory-building and testing -- it generates and empirically tests middle-range theories (modernisation theory, structural-functionalism, rational-choice institutionalism) that comparative politics uses to generalise beyond single-country studies.\n\nSignificance: without empirical theory's rigorous, comparable, falsifiable data and method, Comparative Politics would remain a descriptive catalogue of individual country cases rather than a genuinely comparative, generalising social science.",
