    questionText: "Explain the major features of India's Foreign Policy in the 21st century.",
    modelAnswer:
      "India's 21st-century foreign policy displays continuity with its founding non-aligned principles alongside significant pragmatic adaptation to a changed global order.\n\nKey features: (i) Strategic autonomy/multi-alignment -- India avoids formal alliance commitment, instead cultivating simultaneous partnerships across competing power blocs (US, Russia, and increasingly Quad partners) to maximise its own bargaining space, a modern evolution of non-alignment rather than its abandonment. (ii) Economic diplomacy and growth-oriented engagement -- foreign policy has become closely tied to attracting investment, technology and market access supporting domestic economic growth (post-1991 reform-era shift). (iii) Neighbourhood First policy -- renewed priority on South Asian relations (following the earlier Gujral Doctrine's non-reciprocal generosity principle), alongside 'Act East' engagement with Southeast/East Asia. (iv) Leadership of the Global South -- continuing NAM-era leadership claims through platforms like the Voice of Global South Summit and G20 presidency advocacy on climate finance and debt relief. (v) Soft power emphasis -- leveraging diaspora diplomacy, yoga/cultural diplomacy, vaccine diplomacy (Vaccine Maitri) and space/knowledge diplomacy to project influence. (vi) Great-power balancing -- deepening strategic ties with the US and Quad partners as a hedge against China's rise, while maintaining traditional defence ties with Russia. (vii) Civilisational/exceptionalist framing -- growing emphasis, particularly articulated by EAM Jaishankar, on India's distinct civilisational identity and 'multi-alignment' as a deliberate strategic choice rather than weakness.",
