    questionText: "What are the reasons for the lack of 'regionness' in South Asia?",
    modelAnswer:
      "'Regionness' refers to the degree of coherent regional identity, integration and cooperative institutionalisation among neighbouring states -- and South Asia scores persistently low on this measure despite geographic proximity and cultural-historical ties, for several structural reasons.\n\n(i) India-Pakistan rivalry -- the foundational, unresolved territorial and security dispute (Kashmir) and repeated wars have made SAARC's consensus-based decision-making structurally hostage to bilateral India-Pakistan hostility, paralysing collective regional initiatives. (ii) Asymmetric power structure -- India's overwhelming size and centrality relative to every other South Asian state (unlike more balanced regional groupings like the EU or ASEAN) generates persistent 'big brother' anxieties and smaller states' hedging behaviour (seeking external, often Chinese, counterbalancing partners) rather than deep regional trust. (iii) Weak intra-regional trade -- South Asia has among the lowest intra-regional trade shares of any world region (roughly 5% of total trade), reflecting protectionism, poor connectivity infrastructure, and non-tariff barriers, unlike ASEAN's more functionally integrated economies. (iv) Historical grievances and mutual mistrust -- Partition's legacy, unresolved border disputes, and ethnic/religious cross-border tensions (Bangladesh, Sri Lanka, Myanmar spillovers) impede functional cooperation. (v) Institutional weakness -- SAARC's consensus rule (any member can block action) and absence of supranational authority prevent the kind of pooled-sovereignty integration seen in more successful regional blocs.",
