      "Why is the compromise reached at WTO regarding the Covid-19 vaccine manufacturing not a Trade Related Intellectual Property Rights (TRIPS) waiver?",
    modelAnswer:
      "In 2022, India and South Africa had originally proposed a broad, comprehensive TRIPS waiver at the WTO covering vaccines, treatments and diagnostics, seeking to suspend multiple categories of intellectual-property protection (patents, trade secrets, industrial designs) to accelerate equitable global Covid-19 vaccine production and access.\n\nWhat the WTO Ministerial Conference (June 2022) actually agreed was a far narrower compromise: a decision permitting eligible developing-country members to authorise use of patented vaccine subject matter (manufacturing and export) without the patent-holder's consent for five years, under streamlined procedures -- but this applied ONLY to vaccines (not therapeutics or diagnostics, which were deferred for a later, still-unresolved decision), and it modified/clarified existing TRIPS flexibilities (like compulsory licensing procedures under Article 31bis) rather than broadly suspending intellectual-property protection across the board as the original waiver proposal sought.\n\nWhy it falls short of a genuine 'waiver': it did not eliminate underlying patent rights, left significant implementation ambiguity (eligibility criteria, scope of 'main patents'), excluded trade secrets and know-how transfer (critical for actually replicating complex mRNA vaccine manufacturing), and left therapeutics/diagnostics for future negotiation that has seen little progress -- making it, in the assessment of India and public-health advocates, a much-diluted procedural adjustment rather than the substantive, comprehensive waiver originally proposed.",
