    modelAnswer:
      "Ethnic conflicts and insurgencies impede South Asian regional cooperation through several reinforcing mechanisms.\n\n(i) Cross-border ethnic kinship and spillover -- many South Asian ethnic groups straddle international borders (Tamils in India/Sri Lanka, Chin-Mizo/Naga communities across India-Myanmar, Bengali populations across India-Bangladesh), meaning internal conflicts in one state routinely generate cross-border refugee flows, arms trafficking and diplomatic friction with neighbours, as seen in Sri Lanka's civil war straining India-Sri Lanka relations, and Myanmar's Rohingya crisis burdening Bangladesh and complicating India's Northeast security. (ii) Insurgent safe havens -- weak state control in conflict-affected border regions allows insurgent groups to use neighbouring territory for sanctuary, training and cross-border operations (India's Northeast insurgencies historically using Bangladesh/Myanmar territory), generating persistent bilateral security friction and mutual distrust that blocks broader cooperation. (iii) Securitisation of neighbourhood policy -- conflict-driven security concerns push states toward border militarisation and restrictive visa/trade policies rather than open regional integration. (iv) Heightened regional mistrust -- accusations of states harbouring or covertly supporting insurgent/separatist groups in a neighbour's territory (a recurring India-Pakistan and India-Bangladesh dynamic) poison the diplomatic atmosphere needed for functional SAARC-level cooperation. Together, these dynamics keep security and mistrust, rather than shared developmental interest, as the dominant frame for South Asian inter-state relations.",
