      "Discuss the importance of India's role in UN peacekeeping operations as a ground for its claim to a permanent seat in the UN Security Council.",
    modelAnswer:
      "India has one of the longest and largest histories of contribution to UN peacekeeping of any member state, and cites this record as a central, concrete justification for its long-standing claim to permanent UNSC membership.\n\nScale and history of contribution: India has contributed more personnel to UN peacekeeping missions than any other country over the organisation's history, having participated in the majority of UN peacekeeping operations since their inception (starting with UNMOGIP in Kashmir, 1948-49, and Korea in 1950), with tens of thousands of Indian troops having served across multiple continents. India was the first country to deploy an all-women Formed Police Unit (Liberia, 2007), demonstrating leadership in inclusive peacekeeping practice, and has undertaken specialised roles -- medical care, engineering, veterinary support (e.g., in South Sudan) -- beyond pure security functions.\n\nWhy this supports the UNSC claim: (i) It demonstrates sustained, substantial and costly (in both financial and human-life terms) practical commitment to international peace and security -- the UNSC's core mandate -- arguably exceeding the peacekeeping contributions of several existing permanent members. (ii) It establishes India's credibility, operational experience and moral standing as a genuine security-provider rather than merely a norm-advocate. (iii) It reflects India's demonstrated multilateralism and non-partisan approach (as a historically non-aligned power) that could bring balanced perspective to Council decision-making, unlike P5 members whose peacekeeping and Council votes are sometimes seen as advancing narrow national interest.\n\nLimitation: despite this strong operational record, translating peacekeeping contribution into actual UNSC reform requires P5 consensus (each holding veto power over Charter amendment), meaning India's substantive case has not, to date, been sufficient to overcome the structural and geopolitical obstacles to Security Council expansion.",
