    questionText: "\"India's nuclear policy is deeply influenced by its cultural beliefs and its pragmatic approach to foreign policy\". Discuss.",
    modelAnswer:
      "Introduction: India's nuclear trajectory -- from Nehru's early disarmament advocacy to becoming a de facto nuclear-weapon state -- reflects a genuine, sustained tension and synthesis between deep-rooted cultural/ethical beliefs and hard-nosed strategic pragmatism.\n\nCultural/ethical influence: India's nuclear discourse has always been shaped by Gandhian non-violence and Nehruvian idealism -- India was an early, vigorous advocate of global nuclear disarmament at the UN, framing nuclear weapons as fundamentally immoral instruments (Nehru personally viewed them as 'evil and diabolical'). Even after acquiring nuclear capability, India's declared doctrine reflects this ethical inheritance: a No-First-Use commitment (rare among nuclear powers), a policy of 'credible minimum deterrence' rather than maximalist arsenal expansion, and continued rhetorical commitment to eventual global disarmament -- all reflecting an underlying discomfort with nuclear weapons as instruments of first resort, distinguishing India's doctrine from more aggressive nuclear postures elsewhere.\n\nPragmatic strategic influence: alongside this ethical framing, India pursued nuclear capability for unambiguously pragmatic security reasons -- the 1962 China war and China's own 1964 nuclear test created an enduring security rationale, and India's 1974 'Smiling Buddha' peaceful nuclear explosion and the 1998 Pokhran-II tests reflected calculated strategic decisions to establish deterrence credibility against both China and Pakistan, accepting resultant international sanctions as an acceptable pragmatic cost. Scholars like C. Raja Mohan and S. Jaishankar have described the 1998 tests as an 'exercise in strategic retrieval' -- correcting decades of perceived strategic drift and asserting India's arrival as a great power, a distinctly pragmatic, power-political calculation. The subsequent 2008 India-US civil nuclear deal, converting India's outsider status into de facto nuclear-weapon-state recognition without NPT membership, exemplifies pragmatic diplomatic maneuvering to secure recognition and access despite India's non-signatory status.\n\nSynthesis: India's nuclear policy is best understood not as a contradiction but as continuous management of this tension -- ethical/cultural inheritance shapes the RESTRAINT and doctrine (No-First-Use, minimum deterrence, disarmament advocacy) while strategic pragmatism shapes the DECISION to acquire and maintain capability at all, and to leverage it diplomatically once acquired.\n\nConclusion: India's nuclear policy thus authentically reflects both cultural/ethical beliefs (shaping restraint and doctrine) and pragmatic strategic calculation (shaping acquisition and diplomatic leverage), making the statement's characterisation substantially accurate rather than overstated.",
