    questionText: "What are the challenges and limitations in India-Iran relations?",
    modelAnswer:
      "Despite historical civilisational ties and shared strategic interest in Afghanistan and connectivity, India-Iran relations face significant structural challenges that have limited the relationship's practical depth.\n\nKey challenges: (i) US sanctions -- India was compelled to halt oil imports from Iran after May 2019 under US pressure (loss of the sanctions waiver), removing what had been a major pillar of bilateral economic engagement and forcing India to prioritise its larger strategic relationship with Washington over Iran ties. (ii) Iran's deepening ties with India's rivals -- Iran's growing strategic and economic closeness with China (including a 25-year strategic partnership agreement) and improving ties with Pakistan complicate India's calculus, introducing a degree of strategic hedging by Tehran that limits India's confidence in the relationship's exclusivity. (iii) Limited trade diversification -- despite frequent high-level visits and dialogue, actual trade has not scaled up significantly, and mechanisms for alternative payment/trade routes around sanctions (e.g., rupee-rial arrangements) have seen limited practical uptake. (iv) Chabahar port's underperformance relative to potential -- India's flagship connectivity investment (Chabahar port, intended to bypass Pakistan for access to Afghanistan and Central Asia) has faced slow implementation, financing constraints linked to sanctions risk, and competition from China-backed alternatives (Gwadar port in Pakistan). (v) Third-party hostage dynamics -- Indo-Iranian relations are frequently held hostage to India's relations with the US (sanctions compliance) and Israel (India's own growing strategic partnership with Israel creates some friction, given Iran-Israel hostility), limiting India's room for independent manoeuvre. (vi) Regional instability -- broader Middle East volatility (Iran-Israel tensions, Houthi attacks affecting shipping) adds unpredictability to any Iran-linked connectivity or energy plans.\n\nConclusion: India-Iran relations thus remain constrained less by bilateral disagreement than by the structural pressure of India's other major relationships (US, Israel) and Iran's own deepening China/Pakistan ties, limiting the partnership's practical scope despite genuine shared interests.",
